00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.858
- <i> Hello.</i>


00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.527
This is Thomas Lang.


00:02:41.529 --> 00:02:43.462
<i>Hi. Are you...</i>
<i>Are you in Rome?</i>


00:02:43.464 --> 00:02:45.230
- Yes.
<i>- Oh, great.</i>


00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:47.266
<i>We should meet.</i>


00:02:47.268 --> 00:02:49.001
Okay. Where?


00:02:49.003 --> 00:02:51.303
<i>Um, we could meet</i>
<i>at Cafe Rosati.</i>


00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:52.985
<i>- It's near your hotel.</i>
- Uh-huh.


00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.340
<i>I'll be carrying</i>
<i>a copy of your book.</i>


00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:01.847
Good likeness?


00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:03.882
Not bad.


00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:06.084
- I'm Thomas.
- Hi. Simone.


00:03:06.086 --> 00:03:07.486
<i>Good to meet you.</i>


00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.090
- Have you lived here long?
- Fifteen years.


00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.627
<i>Grazie.</i> This is home now.


00:03:15.629 --> 00:03:17.863
It's not enough money for
foreign press bureaus, so...


00:03:17.865 --> 00:03:23.068
I cover, um, Italy for <i> Newsweek,
Daily Beast,</i> CNN, BBC.


00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.103
- You must be good.
- I do okay.


00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:26.939
You've got family here?


00:03:26.941 --> 00:03:29.007
Yeah. Two young boys.


00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:30.542
And a husband.


00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:34.379
I was sent your book.


00:03:34.381 --> 00:03:35.981
Thought I was going to hate it.


00:03:35.983 --> 00:03:38.116
True crime isn't really
my thing.


00:03:38.118 --> 00:03:40.085
I was flying to New York
on the red-eye,


00:03:40.087 --> 00:03:42.554
couldn't sleep, so I read
your book instead.


00:03:42.556 --> 00:03:44.122
Take that as a compliment.


00:03:44.124 --> 00:03:45.857
Like it says on the cover,


00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:47.019
"Every parent's nightmare."


00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:49.962
- You have children?
- Daughter.


00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.231
I'm separated from her mother.


00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:54.533
I know.


00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.635
How old's your daughter?


00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:00.205
- She's nine.
- And what's her name?


00:04:00.207 --> 00:04:02.174
Bea. We call her Bea.


00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:04.776
Short for Beatrice.


00:04:04.778 --> 00:04:06.311
She was conceived in Italy.


00:04:06.313 --> 00:04:08.193
Her mom was acting
in a film here.


00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:10.349
It's a nice name.


00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:11.984
Yes.


00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:15.153
So, tomorrow's the end
of the appeal.


00:04:15.155 --> 00:04:17.189
Yes, round two begins.


00:04:17.191 --> 00:04:18.457
We'll need to leave
really early.


00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:19.858
Can you be at my apartment
by 7:00?


00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.360
Sure.


00:04:21.362 --> 00:04:23.195
But one piece of advice.


00:04:23.197 --> 00:04:25.864
If you're going to make
the film, make it a fiction.


00:04:25.866 --> 00:04:28.600
You can't tell the truth,
unless you make it a fiction.


00:04:28.602 --> 00:04:31.770
There are so many things I couldn't put
in my book even though they were true.


00:04:31.772 --> 00:04:32.838
Okay.


00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:53.392
<i>In September, 2007,</i>


00:04:53.394 --> 00:04:56.828
<i>two young women arrived at</i>
<i>the university town of Siena.</i>


00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.497
<i>They were looking forward</i>
<i>with excitement</i>


00:04:58.499 --> 00:05:00.579
<i>to spending a year as</i>
<i>a student in Italy.</i>


00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.235
<i>Elizabeth Pryce and</i>
<i>Jessica Fuller,</i>


00:05:03.237 --> 00:05:05.070
<i>both rented rooms</i>
<i>in this house,</i>


00:05:05.072 --> 00:05:07.606
<i>on the edge</i>
<i>of the medieval town.</i>


00:05:07.608 --> 00:05:10.275
<i>Two months later,</i>
<i>Elizabeth was dead,</i>


00:05:10.277 --> 00:05:12.644
<i>and Jessica had been accused</i>
<i>of her murder,</i>


00:05:12.646 --> 00:05:14.846
<i>along with her boyfriend,</i>
<i>Carlo Elias.</i>


00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:18.183
<i>The case made headlines</i>
<i>around the world,</i>


00:05:18.185 --> 00:05:19.685
<i>and the focus of attention</i>


00:05:19.687 --> 00:05:22.454
<i>was the character of</i>
<i>Jessica Fuller.</i>


00:05:22.456 --> 00:05:24.022
<i>Was she the cunning manipulator</i>


00:05:24.024 --> 00:05:25.691
<i>described by the prosecution?</i>


00:05:28.996 --> 00:05:31.163
<i>The quirky student</i>
<i>described by her mother...</i>


00:05:31.165 --> 00:05:33.365
She's smart. She's artistic.


00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:35.067
She's imaginative.


00:05:35.069 --> 00:05:39.604
<i>Or "Jessica Rabbit," as
described by the British media.</i>


00:05:39.606 --> 00:05:41.740
<i>A sexy, drug-taking</i>
<i>student killer.</i>


00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:47.112
<i>On Halloween, Jessica</i>
<i>was working in Bar Rumba,</i>


00:05:47.114 --> 00:05:49.014
<i>while Elizabeth partied</i>
<i>late into the night</i>


00:05:49.016 --> 00:05:50.248
<i>with some English friends.</i>


00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:54.519
<i>Elizabeth spent the next day</i>
<i>recovering.</i>


00:05:54.521 --> 00:05:58.123
<i>The last time she was</i>
<i>seen alive was 8:45 p.m.</i>


00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:03.695
<i>No one knows exactly</i>
<i>what happened after that.</i>


00:06:03.697 --> 00:06:05.597
<i>The only thing</i>
<i>known for certain</i>


00:06:05.599 --> 00:06:08.467
<i>is Elizabeth died a brutal</i>
<i>and horrible death.</i>


00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:13.405
<i>One year later,</i>


00:06:13.407 --> 00:06:16.141
<i>the families</i>
<i>of Elizabeth and Jessica</i>


00:06:16.143 --> 00:06:19.044
<i>- were in court</i>
<i>to hear the verdict.</i>


00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:35.927
- <i> Hello.</i>


00:06:36.863 --> 00:06:39.297
<i>Who's there?</i>
- Is Bea there?


00:06:39.299 --> 00:06:41.466
- This is Thomas.
<i>- Who is it?</i>


00:06:41.468 --> 00:06:44.002
- Thomas, we...
- No. Bea is out with her mum.


00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:46.671
Okay. Then I'll try again
tomorrow.


00:06:46.673 --> 00:06:48.353
- Okay, bye.
- Yeah. Bye.


00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:52.544
You must know this road well.


00:06:52.546 --> 00:06:54.579
Yeah, every week for a year.


00:06:54.581 --> 00:06:57.382
Up to Siena on Thursday
and back on Saturday.


00:06:57.384 --> 00:07:00.819
Crazy Italian legal system
totally fucked up our lives.


00:07:00.821 --> 00:07:02.821
You were covering
this story from the beginning?


00:07:02.823 --> 00:07:04.256
Yeah. Pretty much.


00:07:04.258 --> 00:07:06.858
I was due to go to Modena
to test drive a Maserati,


00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.094
when ANSA,
the Italian wire service,


00:07:09.096 --> 00:07:11.897
said that an English student
had been murdered in Siena.


00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:14.166
<i>Well, I decided to</i>
<i>go take a look.</i>


00:07:14.168 --> 00:07:15.834
<i>I knew an Italian</i>
<i>journalist based there</i>


00:07:15.836 --> 00:07:18.703
<i>for</i> Corriere Della Sera,
<i>Roberto Feluci.</i>


00:07:21.541 --> 00:07:23.141
<i>Look, it's Maria Argento,</i>


00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.610
<i>head of the local homicide.</i>


00:07:25.612 --> 00:07:28.346
That's Polizia,
not Carabinieri?


00:07:28.348 --> 00:07:31.349
I know.
Carabinieri are furious.


00:07:31.351 --> 00:07:33.342
We're getting bits and
pieces of information,


00:07:33.368 --> 00:07:34.986
but we do know that
the victim's name


00:07:34.988 --> 00:07:36.555
is Elizabeth Pryce.


00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:41.460
She was found dead
in the bedroom


00:07:41.462 --> 00:07:44.062
<i>of her house that you can see</i>
<i>just behind me down there.</i>


00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:57.943
Worst part is, I bumped
into them later that day.


00:07:57.945 --> 00:07:59.411
I'm tired.


00:08:01.481 --> 00:08:04.583
Hi. Excuse me, I'm
a journalist from <i> Newsweek.</i>


00:08:04.585 --> 00:08:06.184
You were Elizabeth's roommate,
right?


00:08:06.186 --> 00:08:07.586
That's right.


00:08:07.588 --> 00:08:10.155
- Oh, it must be terrible.
- It's really shocking.


00:08:10.157 --> 00:08:12.858
I'm too scared to go back
in the house.


00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:14.292
I think I put in my notes, uh,


00:08:14.294 --> 00:08:16.394
"She's a dud.
He looks boring."


00:08:16.396 --> 00:08:18.363
I could've had a scoop.


00:08:18.365 --> 00:08:22.200
A world exclusive, instead I left
Siena to go test drive the Maserati.


00:08:22.202 --> 00:08:24.202
- The one that got away.
- Exactly.


00:08:24.204 --> 00:08:25.837
I was still in Modena
when I heard that


00:08:25.839 --> 00:08:28.640
an American woman, an Italian
man and a Congolese man


00:08:28.642 --> 00:08:31.576
<i>So, I turned around,</i>
<i>went straight back.</i>


00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:33.745
Hi, can I talk to you?


00:08:33.747 --> 00:08:35.480
Have you spoken
to your daughter?


00:08:35.482 --> 00:08:39.551
<i>At this point, none of us knew
the names of the arrested.</i>


00:08:39.553 --> 00:08:40.652
Hey there.


00:08:40.654 --> 00:08:42.187
Okay, listen up, everybody.


00:08:42.189 --> 00:08:45.223
In the, uh, Elizabeth Pryce
memorial sweepstake,


00:08:45.225 --> 00:08:46.625
the runners and riders are


00:08:46.627 --> 00:08:49.561
Jessica Fuller, Carlo Elias,


00:08:49.563 --> 00:08:51.963
- and, uh, Cedric Bapupa.
How do you know this stuff?


00:08:51.965 --> 00:08:53.198
Sometimes you got to
leave the cafe, mate.


00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.266
- Even in the rain.
- Okay. Got it.


00:08:54.268 --> 00:08:57.235
Jessica Fuller, 21,
from Baltimore.


00:08:57.237 --> 00:09:00.539
Carlo Elias, 25.
Likes knives.


00:09:00.541 --> 00:09:02.941
- It's like a public confession.
- Yeah, or a suicide note.


00:09:02.943 --> 00:09:04.142
Who's Cedric Bapupa?


00:09:04.144 --> 00:09:06.545
He runs the bar where
Jessica works. Bar Rumba.


00:09:06.547 --> 00:09:08.179
I get scuppered
even in my own town.


00:09:08.181 --> 00:09:09.548
Yes, you do.
You get scuppered.


00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:12.083
Well, I see your meat-cleaver,
I raise you a gun.


00:09:12.085 --> 00:09:13.752
And a YouTube link.


00:09:13.754 --> 00:09:15.186
She's pretty.


00:09:15.188 --> 00:09:16.922
<i>You put this on YouTube,</i>
<i>you motherfucker,</i>


00:09:16.924 --> 00:09:19.124
<i>I'm going to kill you, okay?</i>


00:09:19.126 --> 00:09:20.358
<i>Now, they were unlucky.</i>


00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.060
The families tried to
erase the Internet info,


00:09:22.062 --> 00:09:23.361
but it was too late.


00:09:23.363 --> 00:09:25.897
All their dirty laundry
was hung out to dry.


00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:40.946
Thomas just wanted to meet,
and um,


00:09:40.948 --> 00:09:42.708
get your take on what happened.


00:09:44.183 --> 00:09:45.817
Of course.


00:09:45.819 --> 00:09:47.919
I'm asked many times.


00:09:47.921 --> 00:09:50.322
- When did you hear about the killing?
- A phone call.


00:09:50.324 --> 00:09:51.990
<i>There wasn't much information.</i>


00:09:51.992 --> 00:09:53.391
<i>Just the address of the house.</i>


00:09:57.663 --> 00:10:00.165
<i>The burglary was false.</i>


00:10:00.167 --> 00:10:03.234
<i>Glass on top</i>
<i>of the disturbed clothing,</i>


00:10:03.236 --> 00:10:04.502
<i>wrong way around.</i>


00:10:05.471 --> 00:10:08.206
Most importantly, the body.


00:10:08.208 --> 00:10:10.075
<i>It was covered by a duvet.</i>


00:10:11.043 --> 00:10:13.278
At that moment,
I could feel it.


00:10:13.946 --> 00:10:15.080
Only a woman


00:10:15.082 --> 00:10:17.782
would cover a body
when it is already dead.


00:10:17.784 --> 00:10:19.651
The following day,
I asked Jessica


00:10:19.653 --> 00:10:21.620
to show me
around the apartment.


00:10:22.555 --> 00:10:23.822
She was very calm.


00:10:23.824 --> 00:10:25.056
Very collected.


00:10:25.058 --> 00:10:26.591
When we came to the kitchen,


00:10:26.593 --> 00:10:28.460
I asked her
to look in the drawer


00:10:28.462 --> 00:10:31.363
and tell me if there were
any knives missing.


00:10:33.199 --> 00:10:34.833
She became hysterical.


00:10:34.835 --> 00:10:36.801
That is when we started to tap


00:10:36.803 --> 00:10:38.604
the phones of
Jessica and Carlo.


00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.244
<i>We knew Carlo was lying.</i>


00:10:46.246 --> 00:10:49.948
He switched off his
phone at 8:45 that night.


00:10:50.383 --> 00:10:51.683
So had Jessica.


00:10:51.685 --> 00:10:54.986
They turned their phones on at
6:00 the next morning.


00:10:54.988 --> 00:10:56.921
He had no phone call
from his father.


00:11:04.797 --> 00:11:06.564
We checked his computer.


00:11:06.566 --> 00:11:08.466
He hadn't even surfed the net.


00:11:08.468 --> 00:11:11.236
They hadn't thought
through their alibis.


00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.615
Carlo and I went
back to his apartment.


00:11:23.617 --> 00:11:25.350
We watched a DVD together.


00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:27.819
<i>Amelie.</i>


00:11:27.821 --> 00:11:29.387
And then we had dinner.


00:11:29.389 --> 00:11:31.356
Together?
- Yes.


00:11:33.092 --> 00:11:34.559
Then we made love.


00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:41.499
You got a message on
your phone from Cedric Bapupa.


00:11:42.201 --> 00:11:44.135
You replied...


00:11:44.137 --> 00:11:46.004
- Did you see him?
- No.


00:11:46.006 --> 00:11:48.239
Why did you say
<i>"Ci vediamo pui tardi"?</i>


00:11:48.241 --> 00:11:49.874
I was just being friendly.


00:11:50.876 --> 00:11:52.377
If they were guilty,


00:11:52.379 --> 00:11:54.379
wouldn't they have worked out
what to say?


00:11:54.381 --> 00:11:57.515
People do not behave
rationally in cases like this.


00:11:57.517 --> 00:11:59.384
So she panicked
and accused Bapupa?


00:11:59.719 --> 00:12:01.319
Exactly.


00:12:01.321 --> 00:12:05.156
I met Cedric and took him back
to our house


00:12:05.158 --> 00:12:07.325
because he liked Elizabeth


00:12:07.327 --> 00:12:09.894
and wanted to start
something with her.


00:12:09.896 --> 00:12:12.397
Cedric and her
were in Lizzie's bedroom


00:12:12.399 --> 00:12:14.666
while I must've stayed
in the kitchen.


00:12:14.668 --> 00:12:17.502
At a certain point,
I heard Lizzie screaming.


00:12:19.004 --> 00:12:21.339
I was frightened of him.


00:12:21.341 --> 00:12:23.975
I got scared and put my hands
over my ears.


00:12:25.411 --> 00:12:27.879
I can't remember anything else.


00:12:42.628 --> 00:12:44.796
And why would she accuse
someone who's innocent?


00:12:44.798 --> 00:12:46.064
Who knows?


00:12:46.066 --> 00:12:49.267
Maybe she knew a black guy
had been involved somehow,


00:12:49.269 --> 00:12:50.769
thought, uh,


00:12:50.771 --> 00:12:52.771
accusing a different black man


00:12:53.606 --> 00:12:54.939
might confuse us.


00:13:51.263 --> 00:13:53.398
- Hey, darling.
- Joey.


00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:57.001
Joe, this is, um, Thomas.


00:13:57.003 --> 00:14:01.172
- Currently head of... -Uh, this
week the <i> Daily Mail</i> and Sky.


00:14:01.174 --> 00:14:02.807
- Oh, so, are you the film guy?
- Right.


00:14:02.809 --> 00:14:04.909
Who's gonna play me?
I bet everyone asks you that.


00:14:04.911 --> 00:14:06.644
- No, you're the first.
- Oh, great. Well, hey...


00:14:06.646 --> 00:14:09.047
- I'm thinking Colin Farrell.
- He's Irish.


00:14:09.049 --> 00:14:10.765
Yeah. He's also really
good-looking, sensitive.


00:14:10.791 --> 00:14:11.516
This is perfect.


00:14:11.518 --> 00:14:13.585
Change the accent
and keep the face.


00:14:13.587 --> 00:14:15.153
- I'll bear him in mind.
- Sure.


00:14:15.155 --> 00:14:18.156
Listen. I love your wife's
TV show, by the way.


00:14:18.158 --> 00:14:20.358
She's not my wife.
She's my ex-partner.


00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.660
Yeah. It must have been very
difficult when you separated.


00:14:22.662 --> 00:14:24.229
Especially because
she moved in with her


00:14:24.231 --> 00:14:25.396
onscreen husband.


00:14:25.398 --> 00:14:27.098
Now, would you be interested
in talking about that?


00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:29.200
We could put across
your point of view, you know.


00:14:29.202 --> 00:14:30.635
The difficulty of having
your private life


00:14:30.637 --> 00:14:32.737
- picked over by the gossip industry -No.
I wouldn't.


00:14:33.305 --> 00:14:34.405
It's worth a try.


00:14:34.407 --> 00:14:36.474
- Uh, are going to eat later?
- Sure.


00:14:36.476 --> 00:14:38.042
- Usual place? Uh-huh
- Yes.


00:14:38.911 --> 00:14:40.011
Sorry.


00:14:41.146 --> 00:14:42.546
- Hey. Hi.
- Hi.


00:14:47.686 --> 00:14:49.787
Hey. Hi, Bea.
<i>- Hey!</i>


00:14:50.656 --> 00:14:52.490
- How are you?
<i>- Good.</i>


00:14:53.092 --> 00:14:54.158
<i>Where are you?</i>


00:14:54.593 --> 00:14:55.660
In Italy.


00:14:56.428 --> 00:14:58.129
<i>When are you coming home?</i>


00:14:58.597 --> 00:14:59.931
Uh, London home?


00:15:00.633 --> 00:15:03.134
<i>No. Your home. Here home.</i>


00:15:03.136 --> 00:15:05.403
Um, soon.


00:15:05.971 --> 00:15:07.572
- In a few weeks.
<i>- Okay.</i>


00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.340
I Miss you!


00:15:10.042 --> 00:15:12.911
Missing you, too.


00:15:12.913 --> 00:15:15.546
- What you're up to? <i>Bea, come
on, we're going to be late.</i>


00:15:15.548 --> 00:15:17.215
<i>Okay, coming!</i>


00:15:17.217 --> 00:15:18.683
<i>Well, I got to go, Daddy.</i>


00:15:18.685 --> 00:15:20.485
Okay.
I call you tomorrow, okay?


00:15:21.754 --> 00:15:22.887
Bea?


00:15:23.289 --> 00:15:24.355
Bea?


00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:30.895
- Hey.
- Hi, Sarah.


00:15:30.897 --> 00:15:32.664
- How are you?
- Good.


00:15:32.666 --> 00:15:34.265
- This is Sarah.
- Red or white?


00:15:34.267 --> 00:15:36.000
She's a producer
for the US networks.


00:15:36.002 --> 00:15:38.336
She has a special fondness
for Siena.


00:15:38.338 --> 00:15:41.639
Not. I spent so much time here,
my marriage went down the pan.


00:15:41.641 --> 00:15:44.409
Just another casualty
of war reporting.


00:15:44.411 --> 00:15:46.811
- It's not really a war though. -No,
it kind of feels like it though.


00:15:46.813 --> 00:15:48.513
There's all this competitive
flag-waving.


00:15:48.515 --> 00:15:50.114
It's like they're innocent
or they're guilty,


00:15:50.116 --> 00:15:51.349
and you can't be neutral.


00:15:51.351 --> 00:15:52.417
What do you think?


00:15:52.419 --> 00:15:53.685
I work for the networks.


00:15:53.687 --> 00:15:55.520
We pay for Jessica's
family to be here.


00:15:55.522 --> 00:15:58.122
So, they have spent
an absolute fortune


00:15:58.124 --> 00:16:00.224
on the flights, hotels,
lawyers,


00:16:00.226 --> 00:16:02.393
and we get exclusive
interviews in return.


00:16:02.395 --> 00:16:04.729
- So she's innocent?


00:16:04.731 --> 00:16:06.998
Of course she is innocent.


00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.700
No, look, they have the guy
banged up.


00:16:08.702 --> 00:16:09.867
- Joseph, okay?
- Wrong guy.


00:16:09.869 --> 00:16:11.869
He had a criminal record.


00:16:11.871 --> 00:16:13.271
He had threatened
somebody with a knife.


00:16:13.273 --> 00:16:15.373
Right. So why did they
accuse Cedric Bapupa?


00:16:15.375 --> 00:16:16.641
Uh, why did they
switch the phone off?


00:16:16.643 --> 00:16:18.242
Why did she say she was
there and heard screams?


00:16:18.244 --> 00:16:19.544
And why sign a confession?


00:16:19.546 --> 00:16:21.412
And, uh, the blond hair
that they found on the body.


00:16:21.414 --> 00:16:22.480
Which they lost.


00:16:22.482 --> 00:16:23.915
When the police showed up
at the house,


00:16:23.917 --> 00:16:25.583
she and Carlo had
a mop and bucket.


00:16:25.585 --> 00:16:28.319
They'd been out at 7:00
in the morning buying bleach.


00:16:28.321 --> 00:16:29.487
What were they cleaning up?


00:16:29.489 --> 00:16:31.609
Why was she doing yoga
in the police waiting room?


00:16:34.661 --> 00:16:36.894
Listen. The police
and the prosecutors,


00:16:36.896 --> 00:16:39.964
they condemned the wrong
people in the wrong order.


00:16:39.966 --> 00:16:41.933
- And the press all piled in after.
- That's what we do.


00:16:41.935 --> 00:16:44.102
At the end of the day,
she said she was there,


00:16:44.104 --> 00:16:46.637
in the house, on the night.
Guilty!


00:16:46.639 --> 00:16:48.373
- Game Over.
- Exactly.


00:16:48.375 --> 00:16:50.775
Why would they do it?
What's the motive?


00:16:50.777 --> 00:16:53.244
- You need a reason.
- Right. It's not a film.


00:16:53.246 --> 00:16:55.680
Okay? It's not
a Miss Marple mystery.


00:16:55.682 --> 00:16:58.216
Why does no one ever want to
make a story about Carlo?


00:16:59.819 --> 00:17:01.686
No. No.
Carlo is the quiet one.


00:17:01.688 --> 00:17:03.588
What will happen tomorrow
with the appeal?


00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:06.591
Yeah, we'll get our pictures,
and then we'll go.


00:17:06.593 --> 00:17:08.126
This is just
a really good excuse


00:17:08.128 --> 00:17:09.761
to catch up on all the gossip.


00:17:10.864 --> 00:17:12.430
How's your husband?


00:17:12.432 --> 00:17:14.465
- The last I heard... -Is he still
chasing small boys in Morocco, or...


00:17:14.467 --> 00:17:16.334
Fucking your wife, actually.


00:17:17.570 --> 00:17:18.703
Someone has to.


00:17:29.048 --> 00:17:30.648
Good evening.


00:17:35.788 --> 00:17:37.308
- Well.
- Well.


00:17:37.723 --> 00:17:39.524
I guess it's goodnight.


00:17:39.526 --> 00:17:40.691
Guess so.


00:18:20.499 --> 00:18:22.366
You okay?
You need a pass.


00:18:23.569 --> 00:18:24.635
Thank you.


00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:40.118
Wow, she changed her style.


00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:41.586
They're not going to
make the same mistake twice,


00:18:41.588 --> 00:18:42.687
are they?


00:18:44.022 --> 00:18:45.623
How're you feeling today,
Jessica?


00:18:46.625 --> 00:18:48.392
I'm going to call that demure.


00:18:58.837 --> 00:19:01.105
Simone, how did Jessica
strike you today?


00:19:01.107 --> 00:19:03.441
Her hair was darker than it
was the last time we saw her.


00:19:03.443 --> 00:19:06.043
She was wearing a very
demure skirt.


00:19:06.045 --> 00:19:08.813
She seemed older,
more composed.


00:19:08.815 --> 00:19:10.581
Her Italian had improved a lot.


00:19:10.583 --> 00:19:12.283
And what can we expect
next from the case?


00:19:12.285 --> 00:19:13.651
The case has been adjourned,


00:19:13.653 --> 00:19:16.654
but the appellate process
could take many months.


00:19:26.065 --> 00:19:27.131
Hey.


00:19:27.499 --> 00:19:28.566
Coffee.


00:19:29.801 --> 00:19:31.035
- Hey.
- Hey.


00:19:31.037 --> 00:19:32.470
So, does it all make sense?


00:19:32.472 --> 00:19:34.305
Not yet. Too many angles.


00:19:34.307 --> 00:19:37.275
Jessica is innocent.
That is your story.


00:19:37.277 --> 00:19:38.609
Uh, this is Francesco.


00:19:38.611 --> 00:19:39.944
He's Joseph's lawyer.


00:19:39.946 --> 00:19:42.146
This is Thomas. He's making
a film about Jessica.


00:19:42.148 --> 00:19:43.714
Not about Jessica,
about the case.


00:19:43.716 --> 00:19:47.351
Hmm. You should make Joseph
your protagonist.


00:19:47.353 --> 00:19:49.220
His is the best story of all,


00:19:49.222 --> 00:19:51.022
and it tells you a lot
about Italy.


00:19:51.024 --> 00:19:52.223
Well, about the world.


00:19:52.225 --> 00:19:53.858
Go on.


00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.027
The police knew there was
someone else in the flat.


00:19:56.029 --> 00:19:58.763
Someone had a shit in
the toilet, and left it there.


00:19:58.765 --> 00:20:00.765
So they arrested Cedric Bapupa.


00:20:00.767 --> 00:20:04.302
Then he got lucky.
He got a cast-iron alibi.


00:20:04.304 --> 00:20:07.171
So the police start checking
their database.


00:20:07.173 --> 00:20:10.274
They found that the shit
belonged to Joseph Derma.


00:20:10.276 --> 00:20:13.978
A young guy who had been
adopted by a family in Siena.


00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:15.179
He was definitely involved?


00:20:15.181 --> 00:20:16.380
He was definitely
in the apartment,


00:20:16.382 --> 00:20:18.182
uh, we don't dispute that.


00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:19.917
A couple of days later,


00:20:19.919 --> 00:20:21.953
Joseph went to Munich.
He left Siena.


00:20:21.955 --> 00:20:24.422
The police got a friend
to contact him on Skype.


00:20:42.107 --> 00:20:44.008
So you see? Jessica had
nothing to do with it.


00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:45.676
It was perfect for the police.


00:20:45.678 --> 00:20:47.678
They just swapped one
black man for another.


00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:49.714
Oh, come on,
you know he's guilty.


00:20:49.716 --> 00:20:51.148
There's no point playing
the race card.


00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.117
Joseph went
for a separate trial.


00:20:53.119 --> 00:20:54.185
I believe what he said.


00:20:54.187 --> 00:20:57.121
And he was the only one
who seemed genuinely sad


00:20:57.123 --> 00:20:58.256
by Elizabeth's death.


00:23:35.614 --> 00:23:38.048
Thomas, lovely, lovely,
lovely to meet you finally.


00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:40.084
- Hey. Adam. Nice to meet you.
- Hi.


00:23:40.086 --> 00:23:42.319
- Hi. How are you doing?
- Hi.


00:23:42.321 --> 00:23:45.189
So, we are all
very excited about this.


00:23:45.191 --> 00:23:48.292
We're absolutely thrilled
that you're on board.


00:23:48.294 --> 00:23:50.227
Well done, Steve, for dragging
him back from Hollywood.


00:23:50.229 --> 00:23:51.529
- Pleasure.
- This will be the first time


00:23:51.531 --> 00:23:53.264
that you guys have
worked together...


00:23:53.266 --> 00:23:55.466
Uh, since, uh, the last time.


00:23:56.536 --> 00:23:58.002
So, um, what happened with your


00:23:58.004 --> 00:24:00.237
CIA in Central America project?


00:24:00.239 --> 00:24:01.639
First I got the stars
and no money,


00:24:01.641 --> 00:24:03.174
and then I got the money,
but lost the stars.


00:24:03.176 --> 00:24:05.376
Ah, such a shame.
I loved that script.


00:24:06.344 --> 00:24:08.612
Thomas, tell us, how was...
How was Italy?


00:24:08.614 --> 00:24:11.182
- Interesting.
- This is a great, great story.


00:24:11.184 --> 00:24:13.264
- It's gonna be wonderful.
- Siena is beautiful.


00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:14.218
It's an amazing city.


00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:16.454
Should almost be
a character in the film.


00:24:16.456 --> 00:24:17.688
We have a clash of cultures,


00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:19.356
American, Italian, British...


00:24:19.358 --> 00:24:22.126
And two great parts for young women.
Do you have any thoughts?


00:24:22.128 --> 00:24:24.495
Fortunately, we are now
blessed with a whole


00:24:24.497 --> 00:24:26.363
ream of fantastic
young actresses.


00:24:26.365 --> 00:24:27.465
Carey Mulligan would be good.


00:24:27.467 --> 00:24:29.667
Yeah. She's very beautiful
in her acting...


00:24:29.669 --> 00:24:31.368
Well. I think I have to
work out the story


00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:32.736
before we start casting.


00:24:33.472 --> 00:24:34.772
- Yeah.
- Yep.


00:24:34.774 --> 00:24:37.508
Have you worked out what
your angle is going to be?


00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:41.545
I was thinking maybe
Simone herself. A journalist.


00:24:41.547 --> 00:24:43.427
Might be the right way
into the story.


00:24:43.949 --> 00:24:45.182
What's she like?


00:24:45.184 --> 00:24:47.952
Interesting. Intelligent.
Attractive.


00:24:47.954 --> 00:24:50.154
- How old is she?
- Mid-30s.


00:24:50.156 --> 00:24:53.424
I think that a whole world of foreign
correspondence could be rich for us.


00:24:53.426 --> 00:24:54.492
Sort of an exile.


00:24:54.494 --> 00:24:57.194
- Naomi Watts?
- Chloe Sevigny maybe?


00:24:57.196 --> 00:24:58.462
- Or Tina Fey?


00:24:58.464 --> 00:25:00.431
I love Tina Fey. She's...


00:25:00.433 --> 00:25:03.534
They just need a sense
of how you see the story.


00:25:04.469 --> 00:25:06.370
Sure. Okay.


00:25:06.372 --> 00:25:09.406
You going to work
on the script here or in LA?


00:25:09.408 --> 00:25:10.608
- Here in Italy.
- Mmm-hmm.


00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:13.477
Not in LA. Can't concentrate.


00:25:13.479 --> 00:25:16.580
Everybody knows Katherine, or
knows someone who knows Katherine


00:25:16.582 --> 00:25:18.616
from some fucking party
or other.


00:25:18.618 --> 00:25:21.519
And there's all that shit with
Timothy, custody issues and...


00:25:23.389 --> 00:25:25.055
I hate this fucking prick.


00:25:26.157 --> 00:25:28.225
Did you really threaten
to kill him?


00:25:28.227 --> 00:25:31.495
It makes me sick to think of Bea
growing up with him in that house.


00:25:32.531 --> 00:25:33.597
Another drink?


00:25:35.066 --> 00:25:36.834
- Can I get two more, please?
- Same again?


00:25:36.836 --> 00:25:38.197
Yeah, that'd be great,
thanks, man.


00:25:38.537 --> 00:25:39.603
Of course.


00:25:42.207 --> 00:25:44.708
I was thinking of flying back
to Italy for Halloween.


00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.443
Shoot some documentary stuff.


00:25:46.445 --> 00:25:47.678
Won't cost that much.


00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:50.381
Yeah, it's a good idea.
Go for it.


00:25:58.156 --> 00:25:59.657
Is it in focus?


00:25:59.659 --> 00:26:01.058
No, it's not in focus.


00:26:04.863 --> 00:26:06.497
Pan with her.


00:26:15.041 --> 00:26:16.273
Okay if we film you?


00:26:17.108 --> 00:26:19.310
Uh, yeah, sure.


00:26:19.312 --> 00:26:21.278
We should go back to
Piazza del Campo now.


00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:22.546
It'll be busier there.


00:26:22.548 --> 00:26:24.582
Well, you know what we need.
Go ahead, I'll catch up.


00:26:24.584 --> 00:26:25.649
- Okay.
- Yep.


00:26:25.651 --> 00:26:26.717
I got it. Here.


00:26:37.929 --> 00:26:38.996
Happy Halloween!


00:26:38.998 --> 00:26:41.031
- Can I get a drink?
- Yeah, of course.


00:26:42.067 --> 00:26:43.601
It's what I'm here for.


00:26:43.603 --> 00:26:46.070
We have a special
on zombies tonight.


00:26:46.072 --> 00:26:47.371
- Try one.
- Good, good.


00:26:47.373 --> 00:26:48.505
Where you from?


00:26:48.507 --> 00:26:49.807
Uh, I'm from London, actually.


00:26:49.809 --> 00:26:51.108
- Oh, really?
- Wandsworth.


00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:52.876
- Oh, wow. Know it.
- Really?


00:26:52.878 --> 00:26:55.613
- Yeah, I lived in London for six years.
- Oh, no way!


00:26:55.615 --> 00:26:57.314
So, what are you filming?


00:26:57.316 --> 00:26:59.083
Did you hear about
the Jessica Fuller case?


00:26:59.085 --> 00:27:00.618
Yeah, 'course.


00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:01.952
Is it another documentary?


00:27:01.954 --> 00:27:03.387
- Film.
- Are you the director?


00:27:03.389 --> 00:27:05.189
- Yes, I am.
- No way.


00:27:05.191 --> 00:27:07.791
- Do you live in Siena? -Uh, I've
been here about six months.


00:27:07.793 --> 00:27:09.326
It's lovely.


00:27:09.328 --> 00:27:11.295
Do you need any actresses,
by any chance?


00:27:11.297 --> 00:27:14.131
Um, no. Not yet. But a guide.


00:27:14.133 --> 00:27:15.899
I want to see the real Siena.


00:27:15.901 --> 00:27:17.801
Do you work here every night?


00:27:17.803 --> 00:27:20.237
Most nights, not always.


00:27:20.239 --> 00:27:22.640
I can give you my number,
if that helps?


00:27:23.208 --> 00:27:24.508
Yeah. Sure.


00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:25.576
Cool.


00:27:32.083 --> 00:27:33.183
Call me if you need me.


00:27:33.952 --> 00:27:35.786
Sure. What's your name?


00:27:35.788 --> 00:27:37.755
- Uh, I'm Melanie.
- Thomas.


00:27:37.757 --> 00:27:39.390
Nice to meet you, Thomas.


00:27:44.763 --> 00:27:46.363
- How are you?
- Good.


00:27:46.365 --> 00:27:48.165
Hey. How'd it go?


00:27:48.167 --> 00:27:50.868
Wasn't quite the Sodom and Gomorrah
your book made it out to be.


00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:52.202
Oh, it's still early.


00:27:52.204 --> 00:27:54.304
You got to give the town
a chance.


00:27:54.306 --> 00:27:55.506
Did you get to the red zone?


00:27:55.508 --> 00:27:57.107
Uh-huh. The crew were only
booked until midnight.


00:27:57.109 --> 00:27:59.710
That doesn't go on
until 1:00 a. M.. So...


00:27:59.712 --> 00:28:01.612
Let's go anyway.


00:28:01.614 --> 00:28:03.374
I want to see Siena
at its worst.


00:28:10.955 --> 00:28:12.056
It's left here.


00:28:19.664 --> 00:28:20.898
- Simone.


00:28:26.504 --> 00:28:27.871
What's the matter?


00:28:27.873 --> 00:28:30.941
Are you scared to see me
after what you wrote?


00:28:30.943 --> 00:28:32.242
- This is Thomas.
- Hello.


00:28:32.244 --> 00:28:33.510
I know who he is.


00:28:33.512 --> 00:28:35.412
You want to be careful
with this one.


00:28:35.414 --> 00:28:37.581
She seems so nice
when you meet her.


00:28:37.583 --> 00:28:41.118
Then, when she writes,
she turns into a real bitch.


00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:43.721
- Come and have a drink. -We're
on our way to the red zone.


00:28:43.723 --> 00:28:45.355
Come on,
that's for shitty students.


00:28:45.357 --> 00:28:47.091
You're not students, are you?


00:28:47.093 --> 00:28:48.492
Not for a long time.


00:28:50.128 --> 00:28:53.230
In a murder, the dead body
tells you a lot.


00:28:53.232 --> 00:28:55.933
I saw Elizabeth's body,
in the morgue.


00:28:56.401 --> 00:28:57.568
How?


00:28:57.570 --> 00:28:58.836
I visit the morgue often.


00:28:59.237 --> 00:29:01.205
I have a cousin.


00:29:01.207 --> 00:29:03.440
It's important to look
death in the face


00:29:03.442 --> 00:29:05.709
if you want to understand life.


00:29:05.711 --> 00:29:08.479
That's why there are
so many murder stories.


00:29:08.481 --> 00:29:10.414
Every news program is
full of killings,


00:29:10.416 --> 00:29:12.015
so many films.


00:29:12.017 --> 00:29:14.151
Death is the end of
everyone's story.


00:29:15.453 --> 00:29:17.121
The only thing we share,


00:29:17.123 --> 00:29:19.003
now we no longer
believe in God.


00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:24.862
And so we have become
obsessed with killings.


00:29:25.997 --> 00:29:29.066
Real killings,
fictional killings...


00:29:29.068 --> 00:29:31.135
But from the safety
of our homes,


00:29:32.036 --> 00:29:33.270
where the only issue is,


00:29:34.172 --> 00:29:35.439
"Who done it?"


00:29:36.341 --> 00:29:38.408
Not what it means.


00:29:38.410 --> 00:29:40.511
When we see it on TV
and not in the flesh.


00:29:40.513 --> 00:29:42.780
In the flesh,
when a friend dies,


00:29:42.782 --> 00:29:44.915
we don't even want
to see the body.


00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:47.351
So what did the body tell you?


00:29:48.153 --> 00:29:49.953
That Jessica didn't do it.


00:29:49.955 --> 00:29:51.555
So why is she on trial?


00:29:52.457 --> 00:29:54.358
Police here are the worst.


00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:55.993
Corrupt, incompetent.


00:29:55.995 --> 00:29:57.715
Everyone is
corrupt here, but...


00:29:58.429 --> 00:29:59.863
She didn't kill her.


00:30:00.498 --> 00:30:02.232
I know her. We have talked.


00:30:03.067 --> 00:30:04.401
She is intelligent.


00:30:05.904 --> 00:30:07.871
Not like most of
the students here.


00:30:07.873 --> 00:30:09.973
The students I teach
are idiots.


00:30:09.975 --> 00:30:12.342
Not Jessica.
She has true understanding.


00:30:13.244 --> 00:30:15.212
So you think it was
one of the others?


00:30:15.214 --> 00:30:18.282
Carlo had a collection
of knives, okay?


00:30:18.284 --> 00:30:20.324
Elizabeth was killed
with more than one knife.


00:30:21.419 --> 00:30:23.153
But the police
never found them.


00:30:23.155 --> 00:30:24.555
Because they are fools.


00:30:25.957 --> 00:30:27.191
I know where they are.


00:30:27.193 --> 00:30:29.560
So why don't you just give
them to the police, Edoardo?


00:30:30.795 --> 00:30:32.963
They will try to frame me.


00:30:32.965 --> 00:30:35.165
And they have already
arrested me before.


00:30:36.301 --> 00:30:38.168
They broke my finger.


00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:40.204
I would be an easy target,
like Cedric.


00:30:40.206 --> 00:30:42.172
- A scapegoat.
- Come on.


00:30:42.174 --> 00:30:44.708
No one likes me because
I write the truth.


00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:47.678
I tell the world
how stupid they are.


00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:49.560
Have you already
talked to Cedric?


00:30:51.082 --> 00:30:52.616
- No.
- Why?


00:30:53.585 --> 00:30:54.751
Are you hiding people from him,


00:30:54.753 --> 00:30:57.221
or don't you know
as much as you pretend?


00:30:57.223 --> 00:30:59.423
Cedric Bapupa is
a friend of mine.


